  'quant-33-sharpe-max-drawdown',
  E'Sharpe, max drawdown',
  E'The strategy deck goes to the IC tomorrow. The junior writes `risk_metrics(returns)` and the numbers look fine to her — until the senior risk officer pastes a known-good return series through it and the Sharpe comes out √252 too large. Same bug ships every quarter across the industry: someone scales the mean by 252 but forgets to scale the std the right way. Find it.',
  E'Spot the annualisation slip on the Sharpe ratio and fix the std scaling.',
  E'**Concept.** Annualised Sharpe for daily returns is `(mean × 252) / (std × √252)`. Equivalently: `(mean / std) × √252`. The intuition: returns sum *linearly* over time (so mean scales by 252 over a year), but std scales by `√n` (so daily std becomes annual std at `× √252`). Forgetting the √ on the denominator inflates Sharpe by `√252 ≈ 15.87` — turns a respectable 0.8 into a comically wrong 12.7. Max drawdown''s `cummax / cummin` direction is also a common slip: peak-to-trough is `equity / equity.cummax() - 1`, then take the *min* (most negative). Mirror it (`.min()`/`.cummin()`) and you measure trough-to-peak which is always positive.\n\n**Find the bug.** The Sharpe value is √252 too large. The annualisation needs to scale BOTH the numerator (by 252) and the denominator (by √252).\n\n**Expected.** All tests pass.',
  null,
  null,
  '{}',
  '{"max_hint_level": 2, "do_not_reveal_solution": false, "encourage_tests_first": false}',
  array['quant', 'pandas', 'risk-metrics'],
  true,
  33
)
